    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 153,
        "text": "Welcome to the Science Café. Tonight we're discussing sleep and learning with Dr. Chen. What sleep range are we using for our classroom discussion?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "5a9b1ba4297888f0d9d172918b6e994871d9a878be4621c05accc349837ed79a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 164,
        "sourceEnd": 328,
        "text": "A target of seven to nine hours a night for adults, although individual needs differ. Consistency matters too: going to bed and getting up at roughly the same time.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "ea1ef3f4849dd63360f6a8548d56946c9de42e004c6fafa6c6999e6b23910222",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 335,
        "sourceEnd": 391,
        "text": "What happens to learning when someone is short of sleep?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "e23f9dc3b513e2320072e7502fa81dafacd19b812f1a608d281c4c2f6719aef8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 402,
        "sourceEnd": 666,
        "text": "It becomes harder to concentrate, and mistakes become more likely. One simplified explanation is that the brain replays what we learned during the day and strengthens those connections during sleep. Think of a filing system rather than an instant recording device.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "b9f549051e56ad9d7d00b28b9226e19543725387a93f3f75dfffb5084dd98014",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 673,
        "sourceEnd": 750,
        "text": "So could a student play a language course while asleep and learn it that way?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "2670d65aa12a09cf0cf868e90aa498141425ad671e20bb92d7f662673cfc399e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 761,
        "sourceEnd": 906,
        "text": "No. Don't rely on sleep recordings to learn complex new information. Gentle music may help someone relax, but it won't teach them a new language.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "0dcb8260462c22bdbdeffcae84e03d70f7902d3171c3d8f3a09d294baee8ceb0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 913,
        "sourceEnd": 936,
        "text": "What about a short nap?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "c32c69493e5ef05d9e02781b5d89d2af001b3c580ca105fe182b08b63a9483cd",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 947,
        "sourceEnd": 1126,
        "text": "Around twenty minutes can improve alertness and mood. Long naps may leave someone groggy, especially late in the day. A nap isn't a reason to abandon a regular night-time routine.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "95d09f45c32d79e35971addd25a19d9fc8fd7b753e389d89ab1073d23045c7e4",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1133,
        "sourceEnd": 1196,
        "text": "We also have a caffeine calculation in our classroom worksheet.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "67adc3b5acfac7a3eb15bfcd8cafcd58b205432207430db8849da333a1fafce8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1207,
        "sourceEnd": 1428,
        "text": "Yes. The worksheet assumes a half-life of about five hours. That's a simplified estimate, not a fixed value for everyone. Coffee can disturb sleep when taken late, and staying awake isn't the same as learning effectively.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "e3a98f60753aa88fec10587256e4328e3cdc52bcf294cb4e3c9e9809d315a561",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1435,
        "sourceEnd": 1472,
        "text": "Should everyone study in the morning?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "1425bc7b12c8cf02576111e88c9c90092678689e6c3d4c71d135bfa25f205fb2",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1483,
        "sourceEnd": 1665,
        "text": "Not necessarily. People more alert in the morning are called 'larks'; those more alert in the evening are 'owls'. Choose a time when you feel awake, while keeping a regular schedule.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "70235994097aee72f562be04e21ce8b4a41f923dc4f8644b24bfcc546a1df2e1",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1672,
        "sourceEnd": 1734,
        "text": "What would you include in the bedtime routine we're designing?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b24d64532f8f202edb0348aa0616b7e160616e1362d82f03d55c318ec51d6480",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1745,
        "sourceEnd": 1973,
        "text": "Avoid screens for at least an hour before bed. Screen light can delay sleep. A paper book or calm music would be alternatives. Review earlier learning rather than expecting new information to enter automatically while you sleep.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "98dbecfbfd17e3de42f354bff56c51700d4bd51f106c938dfba9643f12747473",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1980,
        "sourceEnd": 2010,
        "text": "Any final point for the class?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "4af76c78d498e4eef8ca227dbd6c1bc97fa1b5a3b77ff1f80906a2991f30606d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 2021,
        "sourceEnd": 2209,
        "text": "Exercise during the day can improve sleep quality. And remember: consistency is more important than perfection. Keep notes about your routine rather than judging it from one unusual night.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "f36d73f5cf3871f1cc9275880186f8876a89b9041d24a0fa11948866954d0b96",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      }
    ]
  },
